-- title:
--   K(j(q),j(q^N)) is a curve over K when j(q^N) is separable
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and $N$ a natural number with $N \neq 0$. Inside the field $\operatorname{LaurentSeries} K$ of formal Laurent series, let $j(q) :=$ `jqModC K` be the series $q^{-1}$ times the image under $\mathbb{Z} \to K$ of the integral power series `jNum` $= E_4^3 \cdot \eta^{-24}$-type numerator, and let $j(q^N) :=$ `jqNModC K N` be its image under the ring homomorphism `qExpand K N`, which multiplies all Hahn-series exponents by $N$. Assume that the element $j(q^N)$ is separable over the intermediate field $K(j(q))$ obtained by adjoining $j(q)$ to $K$ inside $\operatorname{LaurentSeries} K$. The conclusion is that the intermediate field $\overline{F}_N :=$ `modularFunctionFieldC K N`, namely $K$ with $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$ adjoined inside $\operatorname{LaurentSeries} K$, satisfies `IsCurveOver K`; that is: (i) for every nonzero $f \in \overline{F}_N$ there is a divisor $D$ with $D(v) = \operatorname{ord}_v f$ at every place $v$ and $\deg D = 0$, where a place is a valuation subring of $\overline{F}_N$ containing the image of $K$, different from the whole field, and a principal ideal ring; (ii) the residue field of every such place is a finite-dimensional $K$-module; and (iii) the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega_{\overline{F}_N/K}$ is free of rank $1$ over $\overline{F}_N$.
--
--   This is the statement that the level-$N$ modular function field, presented concretely as the subfield of Laurent series generated by the $q$-expansions of $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$, is a function field of one variable over $K$ in the sense of the project's `IsCurveOver` package (degree-zero principal divisors, finite residue degrees, and a one-dimensional space of differentials). It is the geometric input used downstream in the analysis of the modular curves of full level, for instance in the results on Igusa nodes and level automorphisms.
